 out with G4 and the lead went from -3.2 to -13.3, a loss of 10.2 points. The engine preferred C6. The 14k answered H4 and lost 10.5 points, which put the lead back at -2.9."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "White's A3 lost 10.2 points and moved the lead from +0.0 to +10.3 for Black. The engine wanted B4. It was one of the largest swings in the game, and it handed Opus 5.5 a real advantage."}, {"turn": 31, "text": "Opus 5.5 played C1 and lost 15.1 points, its worst move of the game, as the lead dropped from +7.7 to -7.4. The engine's choice was B5. The comfortable lead was gone in one move."}, {"turn": 36, "text": "After Opus 5.5's A7 lost 14.4 points and left the lead at -23.8, the 14k played A4 and lost 15.5 points, the worst move of its game. The lead came back to -8.3, so the 14k gave back most of what it had just won."}, {"turn": 38, "text": "H2 lost another 15.0 points and moved the lead from -15.0 to +0.0. From here the game was level. The rest of the play lost almost nothing per move, and the lead stayed near +0.1 until both players passed."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "The only program run was libs.py, which parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberty count, flagging groups with 2 or fewer liberties. It evaluates only the current position, with no candidate moves, scoring or look-ahead, so it is a helper.", "stop": false}}